%I A401544 #18 Nov 09 2026 21:02:42
%S A401544 2,4,6,8,16,32,64,128,256,512,1024,2048,4096,8192,16384,32768,65536,
%T A401544 131072,262144,524288,1048576,2097152,4194304,8388608,16777216,
%U A401544 33554432,67108864,134217728,268435456,536870912,1073741824,2147483648,4294967296,8589934592
%N A401544 Numbers k such that the alternating group A_k does not contain an element of order k.
%C A401544 Alternating groups A_n with n odd will always contain an order n permutation, namely the n-cycle (1 2 3 ... n-1 n), as it is an even permutation.
%C A401544 Alternating groups A_n with n even do not contain n-cycles (as they are an odd permutation), so they require permutations of the form (a_1 a_2 ... a_n1)(b_1 b_2 ... b_n2)...(z_1 z_2 ... z_nk), where the n_i are relatively prime, multiply to n, and sum to less than n. Permutations of this form can be generated with two divisors that satisfy the conditions on n_i, namely 2^p and q, where n=q*2^p, q and 2^p are relatively prime, and q > 1. If q = 1, n is a power of two, so no two divisors can be relatively prime, so the only permutation that satisfies the conditions on n_i is (1 2 3 ... n), which is an odd permutation and therefore not in A_n.
%C A401544 If q > 1, we can generate an odd permutation of the form (a_1 a_2 ... 2^p)(b_1 b_2 ... q), which has order q*2^p = n. This can be converted to an even permutation by adding a disjoint transposition, so (a_1 a_2 ... 2^p)(b_1 b_2 ... q)(c d). This method will work for all n such that q + 2^p + 2 < n, which covers all n >= 10.
%C A401544 Finally, in the case of A_6, the (1 2 3)(4 5) permutation is the only form that satisfies the conditions on n_i, but a disjoint transposition cannot be added to make the permutation even, as there are only six elements. Therefore A_6 does not contain a cycle of order 6.
%D A401544 David S. Dummit and Richard M. Foote, Abstract Algebra, Wiley, 2004, p108-110
%H A401544 <a href="/index/Rec#order_01">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (2).
%F A401544 G.f.: 2*x*(2*x^3+x^2-1)/(2*x-1).
%t A401544 Union[{6}, 2^Range[35]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 09 2026 *)
%t A401544 (* Alternative: *)
%t A401544 LinearRecurrence[{2}, {2, 4, 6, 8}, 36] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 09 2026 *)
%Y A401544 Union of A000079 and {6}.
%K A401544 easy,nonn,new
%O A401544 1,1
%A A401544 _Spandan Bandyopadhyay_, Oct 28 2026
